tel 1.0

base beneficence
import soundness

info   a compiler plugin which indexes givens, test suites and tests as they compile, for tools to find later
detail
    _Beneficence_ is a Scala 3 compiler plugin with a small runtime half. As each module
    compiles, the plugin walks the typed trees and writes three indexes into the module's
    output directory, so that a tool can later learn what a classpath offers without loading
    or running any of it:

    - `META-INF/givens/<typeclass>` — every named given of a `Findable` type, by the fully
      qualified name a programmer would import it by. The `frontier` library's explanation of
      a missing contextual value reads this index (through `givens`) to propose the givens
      which would supply it, such as `systems.javaBaseSystem` for a missing `System`.
    - `META-INF/services/probably.Suite` — every `probably.Suite` object, in the standard
      `ServiceLoader` layout. A suite has no main class: the fume test runner discovers
      suites from this index alone, so a test module compiled without the plugin has no
      runnable tests.
    - `META-INF/probably/tests/<source>-<hash>` — one file per source, listing each test, group
      and suite, its place in the hierarchy, kind, moniker, tags and line, so that a runner can
      list and select tests without running a suite.

    A type takes part in the givens index by extending `Findable`:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    trait Renderer extends Findable:
      def render(text: Text): Text

    object renderers:
      given plainRenderer: Renderer = text => text
    ```

    Compiled with the plugin, this writes `renderers.plainRenderer` to
    `META-INF/givens/Renderer`, beneath a `# source:` line naming the file it came from.

    The plugin is enabled with `-Xplugin:` pointing at the `beneficence.plugin` jar, and does
    nothing at all unless `beneficence.Findable` is on the compilation's classpath. It writes
    only to a directory output; compiling straight to a JAR writes no index. Compilation is
    incremental-safe: the entries contributed by the sources being recompiled are replaced,
    and those of every other source are kept, and each file is written to a unique temporary
    file beside it and moved into place atomically, so concurrent compilations into the same
    directory do not corrupt one another.

    Because each module writes its own fragment of each index, a build which merges modules
    into one JAR (an assembly, a fat jar) must concatenate the files under `META-INF/givens/`,
    `META-INF/services/` and `META-INF/probably/` rather than keep only one of them.

    The library has two components:

    - `beneficence.core` — `Findable` and the `givens` lookup, a dependency of almost every
      Soundness library. Exported to the `soundness` umbrella.
    - `beneficence.plugin` — `BeneficencePlugin` and its phase, built against the compiler
      and published as a standalone artifact, since a `-Xplugin:` jar declares exactly one
      plugin.

refer beneficence.Findable
refer beneficence.givens
refer beneficence.BeneficencePlugin

entry .Findable   marks a type whose named givens are recorded in the `META-INF/givens` index
  detail
      `Findable` is an empty marker trait. A `given` whose type has `Findable` among its base
      classes is recorded by the beneficence plugin under `META-INF/givens/<typeclass>`, where
      the typeclass is the given's first proper base class: for `given javaBaseSystem: System`
      the file is `META-INF/givens/ambience.System`, and the line is
      `ambience.systems.javaBaseSystem`. A given declared with a refining body (`given foo: T:
      …`), which the compiler implements as a class of its own, is recorded under `T`.

      What the plugin records, as the declaration changes:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A plain trait: nothing is recorded
        code
            trait Dialect:
              def name: Text

            object dialects:
              given ansiDialect: Dialect:
                def name: Text = t"ANSI"
      frame
        label Extending Findable: `dialects.ansiDialect` is written to META-INF/givens/Dialect
        code
            trait Dialect extends Findable:
              def name: Text

            object dialects:
              given ansiDialect: Dialect:
                def name: Text = t"ANSI"
      frame
        label A given inside a class cannot be imported, so it is not recorded
        code
            trait Dialect extends Findable:
              def name: Text

            class Dialects():
              given ansiDialect: Dialect:
                def name: Text = t"ANSI"
      ##
      ```

      Only givens a plain `import` could reach are recorded: every enclosing scope must be a
      package or an object. A given in a class, trait or method body is skipped, as is an
      anonymous given, which has no name to import. Many Soundness contexts are `Findable`
      (`System`, `Clock`, `Random`, `Timezone`, `Environment`, `Serializable`,
      `Deserializable`, …), which is what lets a compile error for a missing one suggest the
      import which would supply it.
  refer beneficence.givens

entry .givens   the givens recorded on the compiler's classpath for a typeclass, for use in a macro
    `givens(typeRepr)` is a macro-time lookup: given a `TypeRepr` inside a `Quotes` context, it
    reads every `META-INF/givens/<typeclass>` resource on the classpath of the macro's own
    classloader (the class of the dealiased type names the file), skips blank lines and `#`
    comments, removes duplicates, and resolves each fully qualified name to the `Symbol` of the
    given, as a method or a field of its owning object or class. A name which no longer
    resolves is silently dropped, and a type with no class symbol gives `Nil`.

    ```scala
    import scala.quoted.*
    import soundness.*

    def candidates[typeclass: Type](using Quotes): scala.collection.immutable.List[Text] =
      import quotes.reflect.*
      givens(TypeRepr.of[typeclass]).map(_.fullName.tt)
    ```

    The result is the stdlib `List` of `quotes.reflect.Symbol`, since it is used only by
    macro code. `frontier` uses it to propose the givens that would satisfy a missing
    contextual parameter.

entry .internal   the classpath reader behind `givens`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `givens` delegates to it.
  entry .givens   reads and resolves the `META-INF/givens` entries for a type; see `beneficence.givens`

entry .BeneficencePlugin   the compiler plugin which writes the givens, suites and tests indexes
  detail
      The `StandardPlugin` named `beneficence`, which installs one phase, `GivensPhase`. It is
      enabled by passing the `beneficence.plugin` jar to the compiler as
      `-Xplugin:<path to the jar>`, and takes no options. The plugin writes indexes only into a directory output, and only
      records givens when `beneficence.Findable` is on the classpath; suites and tests are
      indexed only when `probably.Suite` (and `probably.Testable`) are.
  refer beneficence.GivensPhase
  entry #name   the plugin's name: `beneficence`

entry .GivensPhase   the plugin phase which collects givens, suites and tests and writes their indexes
  detail
      Runs after `typer` and before `pickler`, over all the compilation units of a run at once.
      It records:

      - each stably accessible named given of a `Findable` type, under its typeclass;
      - each top-level or object-nested `object` extending `probably.Suite`, into
        `META-INF/services/probably.Suite`;
      - each source's tests, through `TestsIndex`, into `META-INF/probably/tests`.

      Then it merges what it found into the output directory with `GivensWriter`. The phase
      may also report two compile errors while indexing tests: _the suite id "…" must be a
      letter or `_` followed by letters, digits, `_` or `-`, so that it can be typed on a
      command line_, for a suite whose `Topic` is not such an identifier, and _this Testable
      has no topic, so a test declared in this method would not be declared for it_, for a
      method taking a `Testable` whose `Topic` is not fixed (write `Testable of "<suite>"`, or
      `Testable of topic` for a type parameter `topic <: Label`).
  refer beneficence.GivensWriter
  refer beneficence.TestsIndex
  entry #phaseName   the phase's name: `beneficenceGivens`

entry .Entry   one recorded name and the source file which declared it
  hidden
  detail
      The plugin's working record, grouped by source when an index is merged. Not for direct
      use.
  entry #fqn          the fully qualified name of the given or suite
  entry #sourceFile   the path of the source file which declared it

entry .GivensWriter   merges the plugin's collected entries into the index files of the output directory
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Each index file is a sequence of blocks, one per source file, each
      headed `# source: <path>` and followed by one fully qualified name per line. A merge
      drops the blocks of the sources being recompiled, keeps the others in their order, and
      appends the new ones, then writes a unique temporary file beside the target and moves it
      into place atomically. With a JAR output, nothing is written.
  entry .merge         merges collected givens into `META-INF/givens/<typeclass>`, one file per typeclass
  entry .mergeSuites   merges collected suites into `META-INF/services/probably.Suite`
      Skipped entirely when the compilation found no suites, leaving any earlier file as it was.
  entry .writeTests    writes each source's test index into `META-INF/probably/tests`
      One file per source, named from the source's file name and the first six bytes of the
      SHA-256 of its path, so no two modules of an assembly write the same file. A source which
      now declares no tests has its file deleted.

entry .TestsIndex   the static index of Probably tests the plugin writes for each source file
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; the format is read by test runners such as fume. Each file begins
      `# probably tests <version>` and `# source: <path>`, followed by tab-separated lines of
      six kinds — `suite`, `group`, `test`, `nest`, `call` and `open` — which place every test
      declaration in its suite's hierarchy by the `probably.Testable` the compiler chose for
      it, rather than by where it sits in the source. A test declared inside an `impromptu`
      block is left out, and an `open` line marks the place as having more tests than are
      listed. Backslashes, tabs and newlines in a field are escaped with a backslash, and `\*`
      stands for a part of a name only known at runtime.
  entry .version   the version of the index format, written in each file's first line
  entry .index     the index lines for one compilation unit
